   "source": [
    "## 8. Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to read this:**\n",
    "- **Break-even CAC** is the ceiling — spend above this and you lose money on that channel at that horizon, full stop.\n",
    "- **Target CAC (3:1)** is a healthier bar most subscription businesses actually budget to, leaving margin for infrastructure, support, and profit.\n",
    "- Channels differ substantially in retention quality (see the retention chart), which is the entire reason channel-level CAC discipline matters — a single blended CAC target across all channels will systematically overpay for low-retention channels and underinvest in high-retention ones.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Caveats of this analysis:**\n",
    "- 
    "- LTV extrapolation assumes constant exponential decay and steady-state ARPU beyond the reliable window; real retention curves often flatten (a \"long-tail\" of very sticky survivors), which would make this extrapolation conservative (an underestimate) at long horizons.\n",
    "- No actual marketing spend data exists in this dataset, so this notebook computes the CAC *ceiling*, not actual channel efficiency (LTV:CAC using real spend) — pair this with real acquisition cost data per channel to get true unit economics.\n"
   ]
  }
